3. AWS Security and Core Services Revision

Reviewed the purpose and common use cases of the following services:

IAM — Identity and Access Management

Controls access to AWS resources through users, roles, and policies.

An EC2 instance can use an IAM role with appropriate permissions to access AWS services without storing long-term access keys on the instance.

Amazon EC2

Provides virtual servers that can run applications and websites.

A working website requires appropriate server configuration, networking, security rules, and a running web service.

Amazon S3

Object storage used for files, images, backups, and static website assets.

Amazon CloudWatch

Monitors metrics, logs, and alarms for AWS resources and applications.

AWS CloudTrail

Records AWS account activity and API calls for auditing and investigation.

Amazon RDS — Concept-Level Learning

A managed relational database service used by applications that need structured, relational data storage.

Covered at a basic conceptual level for Cloud Practitioner preparation.

AWS Lambda — Concept-Level Learning

A serverless compute service that runs code in response to events without requiring the user to manage EC2 servers.

Covered at a basic conceptual level for Cloud Practitioner preparation.

Elastic Load Balancing

Distributes incoming application traffic across registered targets, such as EC2 instances.

EC2 Auto Scaling

Adjusts the number of EC2 instances according to configured scaling conditions and capacity requirements.

AWS Billing and Cost Management

Billing, pricing, budgets, and cost monitoring remain part of the planned Cloud Practitioner revision.


